\n\nThus, discarding T3 is safe.\n\nF2 \u2014 wind \u2014 might be wanted.\n\nBut next player discarded F2, so probably not building winds.\n\nAcross discarded F4, not F2.\n\nSo F2 might be safe.\n\nBut to maximize my hand value, I should go for Full Flush.\n\nSo discard T3.\n\nThen next, discard F2.\n\nThen work on B hand.\n\nCurrent hand after discard: remove T3, left with B2,B3,B4,B4,B6,B8,F2 \u2014 but I must discard one per turn.\n\nAfter discarding T3, hand is B2,B3,B4,B4,B6,B8,F2 \u2014 seven tiles.\n\nThen I will draw another.\n\nEventually discard F2.\n\nSo immediate move: discard T3.\n\nBut is there a better discard?\n\nWhat about B6 or B8?\n\nThey are in B suit, so better to keep than T3.\n\nSimilarly, B2,B3,B4 are core.\n\nF2 is honor \u2014 harder to integrate.\n\nBut T3 is from different suit \u2014 worse.\n\nIn terms of suit purity, T3 is worst.\n\nSo discard T3.\n\nAnswer: Play T3\n\nFinal decision.\n\nSituation: I hold a hand with a pung of B1 and a chow of B6-B7-B8, aiming for a high-scoring flush hand. My unmelded tiles include T3 (Bamboo-3) and F2 (South Wind), which are not in the Dot (B) suit. To pursue a Full Flush (24 points) or at least a strong B-suit hand, I must eliminate tiles from other suits and honors. The T3 is particularly disruptive as it belongs to a different suit, while the B-suit offers the best potential for high-value patterns like Full Flush or Lower Four. Discarding T3 improves my hand's cohesion and safety, especially since the next player has already completed a [T1,T2,T3] chow, making T3 a safe discard.\n\nEvaluation: Key discard candidates are T3 and F2, as they are off-suit. B6 and B8, while higher numbers, are still in the B suit and could contribute to future melds. Keeping B-suit tiles maximizes flexibility for Full Flush (24 pts) or Lower Four (12 pts). T3 has the lowest synergy with my current structure.\n\nReason: Discarding T3 removes a foreign-suit tile, advancing my strategy toward a high-scoring B-suit dominated hand. It is also safe due to the next player completing their T-chow.\n\nAnswer: Play T3"}
