he chow [B6,B7,B8]. Keeping a pair of B8s could serve as an anchor for a pair or even a pung if I draw another\u2014but B8 is relatively common still (3 unshown), though others may be collecting high Dots.\n- **Play B3/B2/B4**: These are all middle Dots (B2-B4), which could contribute to sequences like [B2,B3,B4] or [B1,B2,B3], but I lack sufficient copies. B2 and B3 are only held singly, and B4 once. Discarding any may hinder potential chows.\n\nReason: My best chance lies in pursuing a **Full Flush** (24 points) in Dots (B), given that I already have two melds in B-tiles: [B1,B1,B1] and [B6,B7,B8]. This gives me a strong foundation. Holding onto B6 and B8 supports this. T6 is the only non-Dot, non-Honor tile; discarding it aligns with suit purity. F4 is a wind tile matching my seat, but without a pair or likely pung, it adds little value. Since Seat Wind requires a pung to score, and I only have one F4 with only two unseen, it\u2019s improbable. Thus, removing T6 improves hand cohesion toward Full Flush (24 pts), possibly combinable with All Simples (if terminals/honors are shed) or Pure Shifted Chows.\n\nAnswer: Play T6"}
