ding in Bams\n\nAlternatively, **B8** is duplicated, but forming a pung of B8 would give Pung of Terminals (1 point), and B8 is terminal. Still, drawing the third is uncertain.\n\nBut wait: I already have **two B8s**. If I discard one, I lose chance for pung. Better to keep both unless forced.\n\nTherefore, **W6** is the weakest tile.\n\nAlso, consider fan count: To win, need \u22658 points. Current assets:\n- Concealed hand? No \u2014 I Peng\u2019d B1 \u2192 exposed \u2192 cannot claim Fully Concealed or Melded Hand\n- Seat Wind (F4 pung/pong \u2192 2 points) \u2014 only if I keep it\n- Dragon Pung? None\n- All Chows? Possible \u2014 2 points\n- One Voided Suit? Currently have W (Characters) and B (Dots/Bamboos?) Wait: B = Bamboo? Clarify:\n\nWait! Tile notation correction:\n- 'B' = Dots (\u997c), not Bamboo\n- 'T' = Bamboo (\u6761)\n\nSo:\n- My melds: [B1,B1,B1] \u2192 Dots\n- [B6,B7,B8] \u2192 Dots\n- Hand: W6 (Character), B2, B3, B4, B8\u00d72, F4 \u2192 So suits present: **Characters (W)**, **Dots (B)**, and **Honors (F4)**\n\n\u2192 Three suits involved \u2192 not One Voided Suit\n\nBut wait: [B6,B7,B8] is Dots, [B1,B1,B1] is Dots, hand: B2, B3, B4, B8\u00d72 \u2192 all **Dots** except W6 and F4\n\nAh! So:\n- All numbered tiles except W6 are **Dots (B)**!\n- W6 is the only Character\n- F4 is honor\n\nSo if I discard **W6**, then my entire hand becomes **Dots + Honors** \u2192 qualifies for **Half Flush** (16 points!) if I win with that.\n\nThat\u2019s a major opportunity.\n\nHalf Flush: 16 points \u2014 easily meets 8-point minimum.\n\nEven better: I already have:\n- Two chows: [B6,B7,B8] and could form [B2,B3,B4]\n- A pung: [B1,B1,B1]\n- And possibly another chow: [B2,B3,B4] again (if draw B2/B3/B4)\n- Pair: F4 (Seat Wind \u2192 2 points)\n\nTotal potential fans:\n- Half Flush: 16\n- Seat Wind: 2\n\u2192 Total 18 points \u2014 sufficient.\n\nTherefore, goal: **go for Half Flush**, which requires eliminating non-Dot suited tiles.\n\nOnly non-Dot suited tile in hand is **W6**.\n\nHence, best move: **discard W6**\n\nAfter that, focus on forming:\n- [B2,B3,B4] (already have B2\u00d72, B3, B4)\n- Keep B8\u00d72 \u2014 might draw another for pung? Or use in chow? B7-B8-B9? But B7 is already used (in [B6,B7,B8]), and B9\u00d72 left \u2014 possible.\n\nBut main plan: complete second chow in B2-B3-B4.\n\nCurrent hand after discarding W6: [B2\u00d72, B3, B4, B8\u00d72, F4]\n\nCan aim for:\n- Draw B3 \u2192 make [B2,B3,B4] \u00d72\n- Or draw B4 \u2192 same\n- Or draw B1/B5/etc., but less helpful\n\nBut key: discard **W6** to commit to Half Flush.\n\nNow check other options:\n- Play B8? Keeps W6 \u2192 keeps three suits \u2192 kills Half Flush possibility\n- Same for others\n\nOnly **Play W6** enables this strong pattern.\n\nAlso, W6 is safe: already used in meld by across player \u2192 very unlikely to help someone win.\n\nConclusion: **Discard W6** to pursue Half Flush (16 points) + Seat Wind (2 points)\n\nAnswer must be one of: [Play F4; Play B8; Play B3; Play B2; Play B4; Play W6]\n\nSo correct choice: **Play W6**\n\nReason finalized.\n\nSituation: I hold a mixed-suit hand with most tiles in Dots (B), one Character (W6), and one honor (F4). I have an exposed pung and chow in Dots. The only non-Dot suited tile is W6. Discarding it allows pursuit of Half Flush (16 points), a high-value pattern. My seat wind F4 gives an additional 2 points if retained as a pair.\n\nEvaluation: Keeping W6 blocks Half Flush. Discarding F4 loses 2 points and breaks my only pair. Discarding any B-tile weakens potential chows. W6 is the optimal discard as it is isolated, likely safe, and cleans up the hand for a major pattern.\n\nReason: To qualify for the 16-point \"Half Flush\" pattern, I must eliminate all but one suit of numbered tiles. W6 is the only non-Dot suited tile in my hand. Discarding it sets me on a path to win with at least 18 points (Half Flush + Seat Wind).\n\nAnswer: Play W6"}
